//? Title: StarGAN: One Generator, Three Domains and a Shared Round Trip
//? Subtitle: Explicit native conditioning, source/domain losses and fixed-final weak-result diagnostics
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
One target-conditioned U-Net translates among three disjoint synthetic MNIST
domains. An image-only discriminator supplies raw source patches and a pooled
three-way domain head. A second call to the same generator reconstructs the
source using its original domain code. The native APIs expose those paths and
all fifteen source fields while the complete editable C++ program owns every
capture and Adam write. Worked examples check transforms, disjoint occurrence
selection, stable cross entropy, loss scales and the shared-generator derivative
before fitting. Three fixed epochs reproduce the original artifacts exactly.
The result remains weak: requested-domain accuracy is one third and known-map
MSE about.203, versus.068 for fitting-only target-domain means.
)
\toc

> Share one conditioned generator and an image-only dual-head discriminator

[Choi, Choi, Kim, Ha, Kim and Choo, StarGAN: Unified Generative Adversarial
Networks for Multi-Domain Image-to-Image Translation
(CVPR2018)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.09020) uses one model across multiple
domains. This compact teaching source uses a two-level grayscale U-Net,4/8
feature channels, least-squares source objectives and three synthetic domains.
It does not reproduce the paper's architecture or evaluation. G has1609 values
in10 leaves, D688 in8,2297/18 total. Digit labels and known transformed
target images never enter the generator or the objective. G reads its source
and requested domain; D reads an image alone, without a domain feed.

The round trip reuses G: fake=G(source,target), reconstructed=G(fake,original).
Both G calls contribute to the same reconstruction partial. The two discriminator
heads share a trunk; the domain head uses spatially pooled features. Source
patches are raw least-squares scores. Only the domain head uses softmax.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/star_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(44, 1500);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle a;
  a.width     = 7.5;
  a.height    = 4.8;
  a.font_size = 39;
  d.node("x", {-16, 7}, "Source image\nOriginal code o", a)
      .node("g", {-5, 7}, "G(x,target)\nShared G", a)
      .node("f", {6, 7}, "Fake image\nRequested code t", a)
      .node("r", {17, 7}, "G(fake,original)\nReconstruct source", a)
      .node("dr", {-5, -7}, "D(real)\nImage only", a)
      .node("df", {6, -7}, "D(fake)\nSame D", a)
      .node("heads", {17, -7}, "Fake source patches\n+ domain logits", a)
      .node("realheads", {-5, -16}, "Real source patches\n+ domain logits", a)
      .edge("x", "g")
      .edge("g", "f")
      .edge("f", "r")
      .edge("x", "dr")
      .edge("f", "df")
      .edge("dr", "realheads")
      .edge("df", "heads")
      .save_svg("build/star_method.svg");
}
